Subject: [PATCH 06984/11362] Task 10 A/B: ObjectHistorySaliencePrior
 matched-budget measurement -- honest null at both default and rescaled weight
 (REQ-ARC-FCP-5591-3)

Unlike InertClickSigPruner (exp5602), ObjectHistorySaliencePrior cannot be
measured through OfflineSolver -- action_prior is not a move_pruner concept
there, and no states_expanded-equivalent counter exists on the live
StepwiseExplorer/E3AgentPolicy path. This A/B measures TRAJECTORY DIVERGENCE
instead: does enabling the mechanism actually change which candidates a real
E3AgentPolicy exploration run selects, vs an identical-budget baseline.

Real finding: baseline, the default-weight treatment (change_bonus_weight=
10.0), AND a diagnostic run rescaled to match ColorBlobSaliencePrior's own
tier-score magnitude (change_bonus_weight=2000.0) all produce IDENTICAL
action sequences on m0r0 under a bare (proposer=None) policy -- zero
measurable behavioral effect at either tested weight, despite the mechanism
genuinely tracking real, non-degenerate evidence (confirmed independently by
exp5601 and this run's own prior_summary).

An early informal pre-check (no baseline) misread a repeated-coordinate
click pattern as bonus-induced over-exploitation; the formal three-arm A/B
disproved that -- the identical pattern appears in the baseline too, so it
is inherent to E3AgentPolicy's own backtrack/retry navigation on m0r0,
unrelated to this mechanism. Corrected in the module docstring per this
project's "cross-check surprising results" discipline rather than shipping
the wrong hypothesis.

honest_verdict=complete: object_history_salience_ab_no_op_at_either_weight.
Open question for follow-up, not resolved here: whether m0r0's candidate-
score distribution structurally prevents the bonus from ever mattering, or
whether a bare-policy selection path doesn't consult action_prior.score()
the way assumed. Recommendation: do NOT flip
SUBMITTED_OBJECT_HISTORY_SALIENCE_ENABLED yet.

REQ-ARC-FCP-5591-3 + two SCENARIOs added to spec.md. 6 new tests; 34 related
tests pass.

